.Dd October 24, 2026
.Dt BIBDB 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm bibdb
.Nd create, read and delete entries in a bibtex database
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl f Ar dbfile
.Pq Fl c Ar file | Fl d Ar key | Fl l | Fl r Ar key
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
maintains a small BibTeX database file.
Entries are added from a file or from standard input, printed, deleted
and listed.
Each entry is identified by its citation key: the text between the
opening brace of the entry and the first comma, as in
.Bd -literal -offset indent
@article{knuth1984,
.Ed
.Pp
Whitespace around the key is ignored.
Keys must be unique; entries whose key already exists are not added
again, and a warning is printed for each duplicate.
.Pp
When the database is modified, it is rewritten atomically through a
temporary file in the same directory, and the original file permissions
are preserved.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width 10n
.It Fl f Ar dbfile
Use
.Ar dbfile
as the database file.
Required.
.It Fl c Ar file
Add every entry found in
.Ar file
to the database.
If
.Ar file
is
.Dq - ,
entries are read from standard input.
.It Fl r Ar key
Print the entry with citation key
.Ar key .
If
.Ar key
is
.Dq - ,
print all entries.
.It Fl d Ar key
Delete the entry with citation key
.Ar key .
.It Fl l
List all citation keys, one per line.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh EXAMPLES
Add the entries in
.Pa new.bib
to
.Pa refs.bib ,
then list all keys:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ bibdb -f refs.bib -c new.bib
$ bibdb -f refs.bib -l
.Ed
.Pp
Add an entry read from standard input:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ echo '@misc{torvalds1991, title={Linux}}' | bibdb -f refs.bib -c -
Add torvalds1991
.Ed
.Pp
Read and then delete an entry:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ bibdb -f refs.bib -r torvalds1991
$ bibdb -f refs.bib -d torvalds1991
Deleted torvalds1991
.Ed
